Chapter: LensSeam

 The platform seam. Lens ships to two substrates from
 one source: Target N runs this chapter's bodies natively, Target B
 replaces them at C# emission with runtime-preamble bindings. Every
 function declared here is one seam point: a real Codex definition
 that the csharp plug's Platform Seam Overrides table intercepts by
 name (CSharpEmitterExpressions.codex), rewriting call sites to the
 emitted preamble class and skipping the native body entirely. Code
 that cites this chapter is target-independent by construction - the
 same call is a Codex DB executor dispatch on Target N and a
 SqlClient round trip on Target B.

Section: Sql

 lens-sql-exec executes one SQL statement and returns the result rows,
 each row a list of column values rendered as text. On Target B the
 binding is _LensSql.exec: connection string from the LENS_SQL_CONN
 environment variable, rows streamed one at a time off a
 SqlDataReader. The native body below is an honest stub returning the
 empty row list; the real native handler arrives with the Lens
 service wiring (DESIGN Phase 6), where the statement routes into the
 apps/data RelAlgebra executor instead of a barbarian warehouse.

 The signature is deliberately pure for this first proof. An
 effect-typed form, lens-sql-exec : Text -> [LensSql] List (List
 Text), is the destination: it makes database access visible in
 every caller's row and lets capability manifests bound what a
 dashboard may touch. That requires declaring the LensSql effect and
 threading handlers through the native call graph, which is Phase 3
 seam work - recorded here so the follow-up is not rediscovered.

  lens-sql-exec : Text -> List (List Text)
  lens-sql-exec (sql) = []

Section: Http

 The [LensHttp] seam is two calls shaped for a one-request-at-a-time
 service loop. lens-http-next blocks until the next HTTP request
 arrives on the given port and answers with exactly three texts: the
 method, the request path, and the whole request body, in that order.
 The body arrives with carriage returns already stripped: CCE has no
 carriage return, so the Target B binding normalizes barbarian line
 ends at the seam boundary and a caller splits on newline alone.
 An empty list means the platform has no listener, which is what the
 native stub below answers today: a native caller's serve loop sees
 the empty list and shuts down cleanly instead of spinning. The real
 native handler arrives with the codex.os.net service wiring, the
 same Phase 6 seam work recorded for lens-sql-exec above.

 lens-http-reply answers the request that lens-http-next most
 recently produced for that port with status 200 and the given body,
 and returns zero on success. On Target B the bindings are
 _LensHttp.next and _LensHttp.reply over the platform's HttpListener:
 one shared listener per port, started lazily on first use, and the
 pending request context parked per port between the two calls -
 which is also why the pairing is strict, one reply after each next.
 The reply's content type is application/json, matching the service's
 wire format.

  lens-http-next : Integer -> List Text
  lens-http-next (port) = []

  lens-http-reply : Integer, Text -> Integer
  lens-http-reply (port) (body) = 0

 lens-http-reply-html is lens-http-reply with the content type
 text/html instead: same strict pairing, same status 200, bound to
 _LensHttp.reply_html on Target B. It exists because a browser will
 not render a dashboard page delivered as application/json, and the
 content type is a property of the platform reply, not of the body
 text - so the seam grows a second reply flavor rather than a
 content-type parameter every JSON caller would have to repeat.

  lens-http-reply-html : Integer, Text -> Integer
  lens-http-reply-html (port) (body) = 0
